<section background-color="heading">
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item colspan="38" vertical-align="center">
      <p role="eyebrow">Hegel · Ideas in motion</p>
      <h1 font-size="40pt">What if removing every limit makes us less free?</h1>
      <p font-size="lg">A seminar rule says: <b>everyone is free to speak whenever they wish.</b> Does freedom grow when every limit disappears—or when people create conditions in which each voice can count?</p>
      <hr horizontalAlign="left" width="lg" thickness="md" color="accent"/>
      <p>Hegel’s wager: an idea becomes intelligible when we follow its tensions, transformation, and return in a richer form.</p>
      <p font-size="sm">A lesson in dialectic, recognition, and social freedom</p>
    </grid-item>
    <grid-item colspan="22" padding="0">
      <ai-image background="true" aspect-ratio="portrait" image-style="dialectical-paper-assemblage" prompt="Editorial cut-paper assemblage photographed on warm ivory paper (#FAF4E8), with crisp folded planes, subtle paper fiber and long soft daylight shadows, using vermilion #A13D32, blue slate #345F67 and ochre #B3822D with carbon-black details, no text or lettering. A single vermilion paper path crosses an open adult seminar table, folds through a narrow threshold, and emerges beside two balanced blue-slate profiles."/>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>